          Print help

===== sverb snippet run --help
Run a snippet on one or more hosts

Usage: sverb snippet run [OPTIONS] --on <HOST|#TAG|GROUP> <SNIPPET>

Arguments:
  <SNIPPET>
          Snippet name or id

Options:
      --debug
          Log at debug level and keep recent log lines for the log pane (log files may then contain
          hostnames)

      --on <HOST|#TAG|GROUP>
          Target: a host, `#tag` or group (repeatable)

      --json
          Print JSON (`{"version":1,"data":…}`)

      --var <NAME=VALUE>
          A variable value, `name=value` (repeatable)

      --concurrency <CONCURRENCY>
          Hosts worked on at once (default 10)

      --timeout <S>
          Seconds per command (default `ssh.exec_timeout_secs`)

  -h, --help
          Print help

===== sverb import --help
Import hosts, keys and settings

Usage: sverb import [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  ssh-config   Hosts from an OpenSSH config (default ~/.ssh/config)
  known-hosts  Host keys from a known_hosts file (default ~/.ssh/known_hosts)
  putty        Sessions from PuTTY (default ~/.putty/sessions; the registry on Windows)
  csv          Hosts from a CSV file (label,address,port,username,group,tags; tags split on ; or |)
  backup       A sverb backup
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --debug
          Log at debug level and keep recent log lines for the log pane (log files may then contain
          hostnames)

      --dry-run
          Show what would be imported without changing anything

      --vault <VAULT>
          Vault to import into (default: Personal)

      --group <GROUP>
          Group to import into (a name or a path like a/b; default: the top level)

      --on-conflict <ON_CONFLICT>
          What to do with items that already exist with other values

          Possible values:
          - skip:      Keep the existing item
          - overwrite: Write the imported values over it (backups: merge by timestamp)
          - keep-both: Import it next to the existing one
          
          [default: skip]

  -y, --yes
          Import without asking (required without a terminal)

      --identity-files
          Also import the IdentityFiles of an ssh_config as keys

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

